              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">NOHrD Citius</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Motorised slat treadmill, wooden frame, rubberised slats</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">250 kg (551 lb)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$22,999–$25,499</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1 year; 2 years on registration</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Walkolution 2</h3>
          <p>
            The smaller, cheaper Walkolution is the machine most desk walkers will look at. Walkolution lists
            it at 110 by 64 cm (43.3 by 25.2 in), 20 cm (7.9 in) high in the middle and 26 cm at the front, and
            from 41 kg (90.4 lb) depending on surface and panels, with a 160 kg user limit. It arrives assembled.
            It is <strong>for walking only, up to 3.1 mph (5 km/h)</strong>; Walkolution says it is not intended
            for jogging or running. On 20 October the store priced it at $2,995 without side panels or stool,
            rising to $3,795 with solid-wood panels and the stool attachment; builds with Walkolution's
            TheraFloor surfaces were $5,795 to $6,845 and marked sold out. OCDevel's figures agree on size,
            weight and capacity, but its prices do not match the store's: it listed a base of $3,942.86 on
            11 October and $2,943 in its own spec box.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two more disagreements are worth knowing. Walkolution describes the Walkolution 2 as built from
            industrial-grade German steel, with solid wood only on the optional side panels, while OCDevel
            describes it as solid beech. And the dimensions in Walkolution's technical drawing (115 by 67 cm)
            differ from those in its own description. If the amount of real wood matters to you, ask before you
            order, and order the Wood Edition panels rather than the Black Edition.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Walkolution 1</h3>
          <p>
            The original Walkolution is the genuinely wooden one: Walkolution says it is handmade in southern
            Germany from solid beech and industrial-grade steel, at 140 by 64 by 27 cm (55.1 by 25.2 by 10.6 in)
            and 85 kg (187.4 lb). Woodway's page for the same machine, sold there as the Walkolution Wanderlust,
            gives the same 187 lb and 55 by 25 by 11 in, and both list a lifetime warranty. Walkolution's page
            describes the slats as birchwood in one place and beech in another. It is larger and heavier than
            the Walkolution 2, and Walkolution markets it as both a walking desk and a running trainer. On 20 October it
            showed a sale price of $5,180 against a regular $7,674; the store's stock indicators were
            inconsistent, so confirm availability before planning around it.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Office Walker</h3>
          <p>
            The Office Walker is a newer, lighter manual pad from a small Berlin company: a wood and steel frame,
            polymer slats with soft-touch covers, 25 kg (55 lb), wheels, and a 150 kg (330 lb) limit. Its site
            lists pre-orders from €1,149 excluding tax and shipping; an all-wood surface was a €1,499 tier of its
            Kickstarter campaign. Sources disagree on the footprint (114 by 54 cm on the maker's site, 110 by
            54 cm on OCDevel) and on the warranty: the maker's site states five years on the complete machine,
            while OCDevel, citing the Kickstarter rewards, gives three years in full and five on the frame. The
            site did not state whether it ships to the US, and we could not confirm that units had reached US
            buyers. Treat it as an early-adopter purchase.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">NOHrD Sprintbok and Citius</h3>
          <p>
            NOHrD, the sister brand of WaterRower, builds its machines in solid wood in Nordhorn, Germany. The
            Sprintbok is a curved, self-powered running treadmill covered in detail in our
            <a href="/nohrd-sprintbok/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NOHrD Sprintbok review</a>; a US dealer
            listed it from $8,199 on 20 October. The Citius is the only motorised wooden treadmill we found on
            sale in the US. Two US dealers agree on $22,999 (Shadow) to $25,499 (Walnut), a top speed of
            30 km/h (about 18.6 mph), a 25% incline, a 155 by 60 cm running surface, 215 kg machine weight and a
            250 kg (551 lb) user limit; one UK listing gives a 15% incline instead. They disagree on lead time —
            "made to order, ETA 3–4 months" at one and "typically 2–3 weeks" at the other. Neither is a walking
            pad; both are for people who want a premium running machine that also looks like furniture.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wood-look-pads" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Wood-Look Motorised Pads: How to Read the Listing</h2>
<p>
            Wood-grain motorised pads are where most of the search traffic for "wooden walking pad" ends up, and
            they are also where listings are least reliable. Three examples show the pattern.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Urevo SpaceWalk E4W.</strong> Reviewed widely in a wood-grain finish, this pad's figures
            vary by source: Urevo's own page advertised 2.5 HP where retailers list 2.25 HP, OCDevel reports a
            continuous rating of about 0.74 HP, and published top speeds range from 3.8 mph to 4 mph. FlexiSpot
            sells a "Basic (Wood Grain Design Version)" of the same SpaceWalk E4W, listing a 16 by 39.8 in walking
            area, 0.6 to 4.0 mph and a 265 lb limit. On 20 October Urevo's US product page for the E4W no longer
            loaded, and FlexiSpot's page showed no price.</li>
            <li><strong>Redliro wood pad.</strong> A Best Buy Canada listing for Redliro's brown wood-texture
            model gives a 300 lb limit, 2.25 HP and 0.5 to 4 mph; Redliro's other pads list 265 lb, and an
            independent review of a Redliro pad gives 220 lb. Check the exact model number before relying on any
            capacity figure.</li>
            <li><strong>Maksone wood pad.</strong> Reviewers describe a wooden edge frame, 265 lb capacity and a
            top speed of about 3.7 mph, while one section of its manual gives 300 lb. We could not confirm a
            current US listing.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            None of this makes wood-look pads worse than plain ones; it is typical of the budget pad market. It
            does mean the wood finish should be the last thing you check, not the first. Before buying any of
            them:
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Find out what the wood is.</strong> "Wood grain", "wood texture" and "natural" usually
            mean a printed or laminated finish. If the seller claims solid wood, ask which parts, and expect a
            heavier pad.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the walking area against your height and pace.</strong> Many wood-look pads have a
            walking area around 39 to 41 inches long, which is fine for a slow walk and cramped for tall or fast
            walkers.</li>
            <li><strong>Look for a continuous motor rating</strong> and a weight limit at least 50 lb above the
            heaviest user, and treat a conflicting capacity figure as a reason to choose another pad.</li>
            <li><strong>Read the warranty and the seller's support terms</strong>, and check whether belts,
            remotes and safety keys are sold as spares.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            If you do want to browse,
            <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=wood+walking+pad&amp;tag=sktiger-20" target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Amazon's wood walking pad results</a>
            show what is listed today — a search, not a recommendation. For specific models whose figures we were
            able to cross-check, use our
            <a href="/best-walking-pads/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best walking pads</a> roundup and choose
            the finish afterwards.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prices" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Price Bands and What Each Buys</h2>
<p>
            The price gap between the types is large enough that the decision is often made by budget alone.
            These bands reflect listings on 20 October 2026; prices change, sometimes daily on marketplace pads.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Roughly $150 to $450: wood-look motorised pads.</strong> The same money buys the same
            specification as a plain pad. Prices for the Urevo E4W alone ranged from about $153 to $455 across the
            listings and reviews we read, which tells you more about marketplace pricing than about the pad.</li>
            <li><strong>From about €1,149: wood-framed manual pads.</strong> The Office Walker's pre-order
            price excludes tax and shipping, and OCDevel converts it to about $1,471. You are
            paying for no motor, low noise and a frame designed to be repaired, from a young company.</li>
            <li><strong>About $3,000 to $7,700: Walkolution desk treadmills.</strong> The Walkolution 2 started
            at $2,995 and the Walkolution 1 was $5,180 on sale. What you buy is German manufacture, a 160 kg user
            limit, a long or lifetime warranty, US support through Woodway and very low noise.</li>
            <li><strong>About $8,000 to $25,500: NOHrD running machines.</strong> The Sprintbok and Citius are
            premium treadmills that happen to be wooden, and the same money buys a top-tier conventional
            running treadmill with a longer warranty.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Two costs are easy to miss. The first is a desk: manual treadmills sit higher than a pad and need a
            desk that rises far enough, which is why Walkolution sells its own height-adjustable desks
            alongside its treadmills. The second is delivery beyond the kerb, which is quoted separately by both Walkolution and
            NOHrD.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-in-the-us" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. US Availability, Shipping and Warranty</h2>
<p>
            Most genuinely wooden machines are made in Germany, so who sells them in the US matters as much as
            the machine.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Walkolution</h3>
          <p>
            Walkolution's former US store now redirects buyers to a consolidated store for the USA and Canada,
            which prices in dollars and lists Woodway USA as its North American team. Its shipping policy offers
            free kerbside delivery within the contiguous US, with room-of-choice delivery and set-up by quote, and
            product pages say transit takes two to three weeks after shipping; OCDevel reported six to eight
            weeks overall. The store also advertises a 30-day trial with terms. One ownership point is unsettled:
            Walkolution's product pages now say the Walkolution 2 is "owned and distributed worldwide by Woodway
            USA, Inc.", while the 2022 announcements from both companies, and independent coverage since, describe
            a distribution and patent partnership rather than an acquisition. We found no announcement of a sale.
            For a buyer, the practical point is the same: US warranty claims go through Woodway.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">NOHrD</h3>
          <p>
            NOHrD sells through its own US store and authorised dealers. Its US warranty is short for the price:
            one year from shipment, extended to two years on all components, including wood, if you register in
            the first year, with 90 days on spare parts. Dealer listings carry the same terms. Delivery is to the
            kerb in one heavy package, so plan the route indoors before the truck arrives.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Wood-look pads</h3>
          <p>
            These ship like any walking pad, usually in a single box of 40 to 60 lb. Warranty terms vary from
            seller to seller and are sometimes missing from the listing altogether, as on the FlexiSpot page we
            read. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a>
            explains what to look for and why the company behind the warranty matters more than the number of
            years.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wood-care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Caring for Wood Near Sweat and Humidity</h2>
<p>
            A walking machine spends its life under a person who is warm and sometimes sweating, often in a
            room with a heater or air conditioning. Solid wood copes well with that if a few habits are in place.
            Printed wood-grain finishes need no special care beyond what the manual says for the pad.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Keep humidity stable.</strong> Wood swells and shrinks as the air's moisture changes.
            The EPA's guidance for homes is to keep indoor relative humidity below 60% and ideally between 30% and
            50%, which also suits wooden furniture. WaterRower, NOHrD's sister company, warns that long-term use or
            storage somewhere with high humidity or high temperatures may invalidate its warranty, and says the
            wood will adjust to a room over its first weeks.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep it away from heat sources and damp floors.</strong> Do not park a wooden machine
            against a radiator or heating vent, and think twice about an unheated garage or basement, where temperature and humidity swing
            widely.</li>
            <li><strong>Wipe sweat off after use.</strong> Use a soft, barely damp cloth and dry the wood
            afterwards. Avoid soaking it, and avoid solvent cleaners on oiled finishes.</li>
            <li><strong>Re-oil only as the maker says.</strong> WaterRower finishes its wood in Danish oil and
            recommends oiling once a year with its own care oil; a US dealer says NOHrD's Citius has a hard wax
            oil finish. Use the product the manufacturer names, since the wrong one can affect the finish and,
            potentially, the warranty.</li>
            <li><strong>Expect the colour to change.</strong> Some woods darken with light over time; WaterRower
            describes one of its finishes developing a darker patina. That is normal, not a defect.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            None of the manual wooden treadmills we looked at needs belt lubrication — Walkolution and NOHrD both
            describe them as maintenance-free in that sense — so wood care is most of the routine upkeep. A
            motorised wood-look pad still needs the usual belt and deck maintenance described in its manual.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Recalls and Safety Checks</h2>
<p>
            On 20 October 2026 we searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall database for every
            brand named on this page — Walkolution, Woodway, NOHrD, WaterRower, Office Walker, Urevo, FlexiSpot,
            Redliro and Maksone. None had a recall on record.
          </p>
          <p>
            The relevant warning in this category is about a brand not featured here. On 16 April 2026 the CPSC
            told consumers to stop using Sperax walking pads and treadmills immediately, covering the Sperax Pro,
            Q1, RM-01 and RM-02, after 201 reports of uncontrolled speed, abrupt stops and similar failures,
            including at least 66 falls or injuries, and 573 reports of overheating, fires and other thermal
            incidents. The importer refused to agree to an acceptable recall, so there is no remedy: the CPSC's
            advice is to stop using and dispose of them, so check the brand of any second-hand pad, whatever
            its finish.
          </p>
          <p>
            Manual wooden treadmills avoid the electrical risks entirely, but they have their own: a curved,
            self-powered surface can keep moving briefly if you step off mid-stride, and a heavy wooden machine needs
            two people or more to move. On a motorised pad, keep the safety key or auto-stop in use, and check for
            recalls again using the exact model number before buying a used one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Who Should Buy a Wooden Walking Pad, and Who Should Not</h2>
<h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">A wood-look motorised pad suits you if</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>you want an ordinary walking pad and the finish matters because it lives in view;</li>
            <li>you have found one whose belt length, motor rating, capacity and warranty would pass the same
            checks as any other pad; and</li>
            <li>you are not paying a premium for the finish alone.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">A solid-wood manual treadmill suits you if</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>you will walk at a desk for long stretches most working days, and noise matters — a shared
            office, calls, a sleeping household;</li>
            <li>you are comfortable with walking speeds only, up to about 3 mph on the Walkolution 2;</li>
            <li>you have, or will buy, a desk tall enough for a machine that sits higher than a pad; and</li>
            <li>you value a machine that should last for many years enough to pay roughly ten times the price of a
            good pad.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Buy something else if</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>you want to run.</strong> Walking pads and manual desk treadmills are not running
            machines. A wooden curved treadmill such as the Sprintbok is, but it is a different purchase from a desk walker, compared with its rivals in our Sprintbok review.</li>
            <li><strong>the machine has to slide under a sofa or bed.</strong> The wooden manual machines are
            large and heavy, and the Walkolution 1 at 187 lb is not something you move daily.</li>
            <li><strong>you are unsure you will use it.</strong> Start with a well-reviewed ordinary pad, build the
            habit using our <a href="/treadmill-desk-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill desk guide</a>,
            and upgrade later if you find yourself walking for hours a day.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Walkolution also markets its surfaces as improving posture and activating stabilising muscles. Those
            are manufacturer claims we have not assessed, and nothing on this page is medical advice; if you have
            a joint, balance or heart condition, ask a clinician before starting long daily walking sessions on any
            machine.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a wooden walking pad actually made of wood?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not entirely. Most motorised pads sold as wooden have a wood-grain or wood-texture finish on the housing, often printed or laminated, over an ordinary belt and deck. Genuinely wooden machines, such as the Walkolution 1 in solid beech and steel or NOHrD’s solid-wood treadmills, are motorless or premium machines costing thousands.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a wooden treadmill cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On 20 October 2026, wood-look motorised pads cost roughly the same as plain pads, about $150 to $450. The Walkolution 2 manual desk treadmill was $2,995 to $3,795, the Walkolution 1 $5,180 on sale, the NOHrD Sprintbok from about $8,199 and the motorised NOHrD Citius $22,999 to $25,499. Prices change.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a wooden walking pad quieter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only when it has no motor. Manual wooden treadmills are quiet because there is nothing to whine; Walkolution claims under 35 dB. A wood-grain housing on a motorised pad does not reduce motor or belt noise, so judge it on the same noise checks as any other pad.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you run on a wooden walking pad?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not on most. Wood-look motorised pads typically top out around 4 mph, and Walkolution says the Walkolution 2 is for walking only, up to 3.1 mph. If you want to run on a wooden machine, the options are running treadmills such as NOHrD’s curved Sprintbok or motorised Citius.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I buy a Walkolution in the US?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Walkolution sells through a USA and Canada store priced in dollars, with Woodway USA as its North American team, free kerbside delivery in the contiguous US, and a 30-day trial with terms. On 20 October 2026 the Walkolution 2 started at $2,995. It is not sold on Amazon.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I look after the wood on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Keep the room’s humidity stable, ideally between 30% and 50%, keep the machine away from radiators and damp floors, wipe sweat off with a barely damp cloth and dry it, and re-oil only with the product and schedule the manufacturer names. Printed wood-grain finishes need no special care.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Wooden Walking Pad</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The mistakes here come from treating the wood as the specification. These are the four to avoid.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying extra for a wood-grain finish</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A printed wood effect on a motorised pad changes nothing about how it walks or lasts. If a wood-look pad costs more than a plain pad with the same belt, motor and capacity, buy the plain one or keep looking. Compare specifications first and choose the finish last.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a manual desk treadmill expecting a workout machine</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The Walkolution 2 is rated for walking only, up to 3.1 mph, and a motorless curved surface makes you set the pace. If you also want brisk incline walking or running, budget for a separate treadmill or choose a running machine instead of a desk walker.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the desk and the delivery</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A manual wooden treadmill sits higher than a pad, so your desk must rise further, and the machine arrives at the kerb in a heavy carton. Measure your desk’s maximum height, plan who will carry the machine indoors, and ask for a room-of-choice quote before ordering.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting solid wood in a damp or hot room</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">An unheated garage, a humid basement or a spot against a radiator can crack or warp the wood, and WaterRower warns such conditions may void its warranty. Keep the machine indoors at stable humidity, ideally 30% to 50%, and away from heat sources.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

